1. Execute this query on the primary server
This query checks the WAL replication lag for both standby servers.
SELECT
    client_addr,
    state,
    sync_state,

    ROUND(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), sent_lsn) / 1024 / 1024, 2)
        AS unsent_mb,

    ROUND(pg_wal_lsn_diff(sent_lsn, write_lsn) / 1024 / 1024, 2)
        AS sent_not_written_mb,

    ROUND(pg_wal_lsn_diff(write_lsn, flush_lsn) / 1024 / 1024, 2)
        AS written_not_flushed_mb,

    ROUND(pg_wal_lsn_diff(flush_lsn, replay_lsn) / 1024 / 1024, 2)
        AS flushed_not_replayed_mb,

    ROUND(pg_wal_lsn_diff(pg_current_wal_lsn(), replay_lsn) / 1024 / 1024, 2)
        AS total_replication_lag_mb

FROM pg_stat_replication
ORDER BY client_addr;


2. Check whether WAL replay is progressing:- 

SELECT
    pg_is_in_recovery() AS is_standby,
    pg_last_wal_receive_lsn() AS received_lsn,
    pg_last_wal_replay_lsn() AS replayed_lsn,
    pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp() AS last_replay_time;

